INT. PAROLE OFFICE - DAY

ON CAMERA: handheld, slightly too close, a fluorescent hum. A wall of grey filing cabinets. THE WOMAN, 30s, sits across from a desk, hands flat on her knees, avoiding the lens. KIM, 50s, parole officer, reads a folder. Beside her a plastic nameplate reads KIP DAVIS, PO II. Kim is not Kip. Kip is the one leaning in the doorway, eating a bagel.

KIM
Camera stays off the client please. We agreed.

KIP
Its a documentary Kim. Everybody signed.

KIM
Not her.

THE WOMAN
I signed.

KIM
You signed a form that said "a person". You didn't know it'd be this.

THE WOMAN
I didn't know a lot of things when I signed stuff.

KIM
Eleven months clean, curfew met, job at the laundromat, check ins on time. Honestly Jen you're the best file I've got this quarter and I've been dreading this meeting all week.

KIP
Jen. There it is. Finally a name.

JEN
Dreading why?

KIM
Because your sponsor called me. Because he said he wants to put his name on your housing form. As your partner.

JEN
Mo.

KIM
Mo Haddad. Who has a record. Who's two years out himself. Associating with known offenders is a violation, you know that, I know that, Kip knows that.

KIP
(mouth full)
I know that.

JEN
So violate me.

KIM
Don't make this a joke.

JEN
I'm not joking. I love him. I've never said that out loud in this building. It's the first thing I've said in here that wasn't a lie or an excuse.

Silence. Kim closes the folder. The camera shakes, someone has stopped breathing behind it.

KIM
Sign here. And here. I never saw the second page.

KIP
Neither did the camera.
